WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.575
HTTP responses are input.

2
00:00:01.575 --> 00:00:09.843
A successful connection or a 200 status code does not automatically make the response safe, complete, correctly typed, or semantically trusted.

3
00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:18.111
The client should validate status codes, content type, expected schema, character encoding, and application-level meaning before using a response to

4
00:00:18.111 --> 00:00:19.292
update internal state.

5
00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:21.654
Resource limits belong in response handling.

6
00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:28.347
A response body can be larger than expected, arrive slowly, use compression, or contain nested structured data.

7
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:35.434
Clients should set maximum response sizes where practical, prefer streaming for large downloads, constrain decompression behavior, and avoid

8
00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.009
buffering entire bodies blindly.

9
00:00:37.009 --> 00:00:42.521
Allocation decisions should happen after the client has enough information to bound the work.

10
00:00:42.521 --> 00:00:44.883
Parsing should be structured and defensive.

11
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:51.577
Use maintained parsers for JSON, XML, headers, archives, or other formats instead of ad hoc text slicing.

12
00:00:51.577 --> 00:00:59.057
Validate required fields, numeric ranges, enum values, version fields, and cross-field relationships before converting remote data into trusted

13
00:00:59.057 --> 00:00:59.845
application objects.

14
00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:03.782
Parser failures should leave the application in a known state.

15
00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:05.750
File downloads need special policy.

16
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:13.231
The client should define allowed content types, destination paths, overwrite behavior, temporary file handling, size limits, integrity checks where

17
00:01:13.231 --> 00:01:16.381
required by policy, and cleanup for partial files.

18
00:01:16.381 --> 00:01:22.680
A failed or cancelled download should not leave confusing artifacts that later code treats as complete.

19
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.192
The safe mental model is consistent: responses come from outside the local trust boundary.

20
00:01:28.192 --> 00:01:36.460
Read them with limits, parse them with maintained components, validate their meaning, and only then use them in trusted application flows.

21
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:44.334
Include failure cases in tests, especially oversized bodies, unexpected content types, invalid encodings, partial downloads, and remote data that is

22
00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:50.240
well-formed but outside the application contract or current service version expectations and limits clearly.
